-- title:
--   Automorphic function field of a cocompact Fuchsian group is a curve
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$ all of whose elements have determinant one, carrying the discrete topology, and assume $\Gamma$ acts cocompactly on the upper half plane in the sense that there is a compact set $K \subseteq \mathbb{H}$ meeting every $\Gamma$-orbit: for each $\tau \in \mathbb{H}$ there is $\gamma \in \Gamma$ with $\gamma \cdot \tau \in K$. Let $\mathcal{K}(\Gamma) =$ [`ModularCurve.automorphicField`](def/ModularCurve_AutomorphicField.html#L150) $\Gamma$ be the subfield of the fraction field of the $\mathbb{C}$-algebra of holomorphic (i.e. `MDifferentiable`) functions $\mathbb{H} \to \mathbb{C}$ consisting of the quotients $g/h$ with $g, h$ modular forms for $\Gamma$ of a common weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}$ and $h \neq 0$. Assume $\mathcal{K}(\Gamma)$ contains an element outside the image of $\mathbb{C}$. Then three things hold. First, [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15) $\mathbb{C}\ \mathcal{K}(\Gamma)$: every nonzero $f \in \mathcal{K}(\Gamma)$ has a divisor of degree $0$ whose value at each place $v$ is $v.\mathrm{ord}\,f$, where a place is a proper valuation subring of $\mathcal{K}(\Gamma)$ containing $\mathbb{C}$ and which is a principal ideal ring; the residue field of every place is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{C}$; and the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{\mathcal{K}(\Gamma)/\mathbb{C}}$ is free of rank one over $\mathcal{K}(\Gamma)$. Second, $\mathcal{K}(\Gamma)$ is essentially of finite type over $\mathbb{C}$. Third, there is $x \in \mathcal{K}(\Gamma)$ transcendental over $\mathbb{C}$ such that $\mathcal{K}(\Gamma)$ is finite-dimensional over the subfield $\mathbb{C}(x)$ generated by $x$.
--
--   This is the classical statement that the field of automorphic functions of a discrete cocompact group of determinant-one real matrices is an algebraic function field of one variable over $\mathbb{C}$, here in the divisor-theoretic form used by the project's notion of a curve over a field. It feeds the construction of the uniformized Hecke curve attached to such a group and the associated statement on points of the curve.
